In Docker, we create a network so that multiple containers can communicate with each other easily and securely.

Simple example

Suppose you have:

spring-app → your Java application
mysql → database
redis → cache

We create a Docker network:

docker network create my-network

Then run containers on it:

docker run --network my-network --name mysql mysql
docker run --network my-network --name spring-app my-app

Now spring-app can connect to MySQL using the container name:

mysql:3306

instead of using an IP address.

Why do we use Docker Network?
Container-to-container communication
Containers can communicate with each other.

Service discovery
Docker DNS allows us to use container names like:

mysql:3306
redis:6379
Isolation
Containers on one network can be separated from containers on another network.
No hardcoded IPs
Container IPs can change, but the container/service name remains usable.
Interview answer

We create a Docker network to allow containers to communicate with each other securely using service/container names instead of hardcoded IP addresses,
while also providing network isolation.